A||||7||Kevin Larke||1985 4 cyl engine runs lousy||||1||
Z||000000||Kevin Larke||03-27-2000||10:54 PM||||I have a white '85 2M4 with 76k miles. I put it in storage 4/91 with 73k miles. I get a 10 day registration every summer so I can burn the old gas (with "StaBil"), change the oil, etc.  Last year, the engine kept jerking and missing at steady cruise speeds. It seemed smoother at full throttle while accelerating, but as soon as it was backed off to cruise, it felt like someone was turning off the ignition about 5 times a minute for fractions of a second. The only code it had was for the TPS, which I replaced. I also put in a new O2 sensor and fuel filter. This year, it's running even worse. It starts right up, but the exhaust "pops" during idle and the jerking is pretty bad, it even happens at full throttle now. I read on another web site that the plastic vacuum tubes can get old and break. Everything looks OK to me. It acts like it's running lean or something. If anyone has ideas, please let me know. Thanks!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||03-27-2000||11:14 PM||||replace the plugs, wires, dist cap, and rotors. make sure there are no vacuum leaks.<P>replace the ignition module and check timing. use heat sink compound on the module. you can get compound at radio shack. it's better than plain dielectric grease.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Kevin Larke||03-31-2000||10:47 PM||||Any more ideas out there? It doesn't seem like a spark plug wire or ignition problem. The vacuum leak sounds more likely. Any tips on finding the leak? Someone told me to use gas from the nozzle of a propane torch (not lit) and "squirt" the gas around the intake manifold & vacuum lines until the idle smooths out. Is this possible? What about the danger aspect?||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||03-31-2000||11:08 PM||||could be that. yes a torch works. a little spray injector cleaner is easier to control. there's not that many things to check on the 4 cyl. make sure both ends of the PCV line are checked, the rubber elbows get shitty. Replace the PCV valve. disconect the vapor can lines from the TBI and cap the TBI ports. check the EGR line and valve. If the EGR base is loose replace the gasket, and the EGR if it's in rough shape.<P>could be a number of things. even a bad catalyst.<P>once you do get it running put some miles on at highway speeds. you want to heat things up good to drive off water and prevent carbon.||||reg||1
Z||000004||BAM-BAM||03-31-2000||11:45 PM||||   You would be surprised what symptoms can arise from plugs and wires.<P> Bam Out!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000005||lowCG||04-01-2000||12:01 AM||||Or water in the gas.I'd run a couple tankfuls through it,and maybe even drain the tank.That additive/alcohol stuff all has a saturation point,and when that's reached,it's the same as not having any additive at all.||||reg||1
Z||000006||grizco||04-01-2000||01:16 AM||||Check your distributor. It sounds real similar to the problem that I had with mine  last summer. With mine the bearings in the distributor were bad and would allow the gear to slop back and forth wich would change the timing. Mine would miss fire real bad between about 2000 and 3000 rpm. I also had the codes for the tps and O2 snsor and changing these had no effect on the way the car ran until I got a rebuilt distributor, it was cheaper than getting the parts to rebuild it myself. Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Kevin Larke||04-25-2000||10:47 PM||||The Ogre and Bam-Bam were right. I put on a new set of spark plug wires, distributor cap and rotor. I looked at the AC Rapid Fire plugs, but they looked like new (only had about 800 miles on them).<P>It runs like new! Smooth as could be. The symptom that made me try this was caused by 3 days of rain. It was running pretty good until the wet weather, then it ran really crappy again. It jerked a lot when it was cold in the morning, then got a little better. <P>I'm pretty sure all the parts were original from 1985 with 76k miles. The dist. cap was tan with a tan rotor. The wires say "Delcore". I couldn't see any visible cracks in the old cap, but the terminals inside had some crusty white corrosion. The cap also had some yellowish-white chunks or crystals inside.<P>I only spent $30 on the parts, but it really made a huge difference in the way it runs. The idle is also much more stable.<P>Thank agains for the tips. My two week registration expired, so it's back in the garage til 2001, except for a few quick 8 mile trips down the road once in a while. I changed the oil and filter (Mobil 1 10w-30) and added 6 ounces of "Sta-Bil" to a full tank of premium.<P>Is anyone else out there storing a factory stock Fiero until it's a classic? I wish I would have put less than 76k miles on mine, but it's too late now. ||||reg||14
